    {
      "heading": "Introduction: The Living System of Generations",
      "paragraphs": [
        "In traditional Murle society, a person's identity was connected not only to their family and clan but also to their generation.",
        "Generations created a social system that organized responsibilities, relationships, leadership, and the passing of knowledge from one period of history to another.",
        "The age-set system was more than a way of grouping people by age. It was a cultural institution that helped prepare young people for adulthood and guide them throughout life."
      ]
    },
    {
      "heading": "What Is an Age Set?",
      "paragraphs": [
        "An age set is a group of people who enter a similar stage of life together and share a common generational identity.",
        "Members of the same age set grow up knowing that they belong to one group with shared responsibilities and experiences.",
        "The relationship between members can continue throughout their lives, creating a strong bond between people of the same generation."
      ]
    },
    {
      "heading": "The Purpose of Age Sets in Murle Society",
      "paragraphs": [
        "Traditional societies needed systems that could organize communities, especially in areas where people depended on livestock, land, and cooperation for survival.",
        "The age-set system helped create order by teaching people what was expected from them at different stages of life.",
        "It helped communities identify who was responsible for protecting, leading, teaching, and supporting society."
      ]
    },
    {
      "heading": "Generations as Historical Memory",
      "paragraphs": [
        "Every generation carries memories of the period in which it lived.",
        "Elders remember the experiences of their generation, including important events, challenges, achievements, and lessons.",
        "Through generations, history is preserved even when it is not written in books."
      ]
    },
    {
      "heading": "The Journey From Childhood to Adulthood",
      "paragraphs": [
        "A young person's life in traditional Murle society involved learning through participation.",
        "Children learned from parents, relatives, elders, and the community around them.",
        "As they grew older, they gradually received more responsibilities connected to family, livestock, and society."
      ]
    },
    {
      "heading": "Responsibilities Connected to Generations",
      "paragraphs": [
        "Each generation had expectations placed upon it.",
        "Young people were expected to learn discipline, respect, courage, and responsibility.",
        "Adults were expected to support families and contribute to community stability.",
        "Elders were expected to provide wisdom, guidance, and knowledge."
      ]
    },
    {
      "heading": "The Relationship Between Younger and Older Generations",
      "paragraphs": [
        "Respect between generations was one of the important values of traditional Murle life.",
        "Older people carried experience and knowledge, while younger people carried energy and the future of the community.",
        "The relationship between them created continuity between the past and the future."
      ]
    },
    {
      "heading": "The Murle Generation Names",
      "paragraphs": [
        "Murle generations are remembered through specific names that identify different periods of community history.",
        "These names carry meaning and represent the identity of people who belonged to those generations.",
        "The complete explanation of each generation requires knowledge preserved by elders because oral history remains the main source for many traditional details."
      ]
    },
